<!--
  Only in the `rtl` build type. Not in debug, not in release, not in the AAB, and it cannot
  reach a user.

  `./gradlew installRtl` gives a build that runs entirely right-to-left. AppLocale reads
  app_language_tag and pins the whole configuration to it, so overriding the tag here turns
  layout direction, digits and date formatting to Arabic conventions everywhere at once —
  which is exactly what will happen on its own the day values-ar/ ships and an Arabic phone
  resolves the tag to "ar". It previews that day. It is not a translation: every word stays
  English, and the untranslated English is the point, since a Latin word inside an RTL line
  is precisely what does not survive the trip.

  It is `ur`, and it used to be the reserved pseudolocale `ar-XB`. That was changed on
  2 Aug 2026, deliberately overturning the decision recorded in HANDOVER §10 ("The
  pseudolocale hides the answer it is being asked for"), which said to stay on ar-XB.

  Why the reversal. ar-XB reverses Latin characters outright — "PM" renders "MP", "Sun"
  renders "nuS". No real locale does that, so every screen looks mangled whether or not
  anything is wrong, and the one question this build exists to answer — *which of these
  would still be wrong in a genuine RTL language?* — is the one it cannot answer. The owner
  reviewed it twice and both times reported the app as broken when it was not. A preview
  its only human reviewer cannot read has failed at its single job, and worse, it spends
  trust: it teaches him that RTL reports are noise.

  `ur` instead of `ar` on purpose. Urdu is right-to-left, keeps Latin AM/PM in CLDR, and
  uses Latin digits, so prayer times stay legible while the hardest case — a Latin run
  inside an RTL line — is still exercised. `ar` would switch to Arabic-Indic digits and
  make every time unreadable to this project's reviewer, testing less and hiding more.

  Nothing here is a translation. There is no values-ur/, so every string still resolves
  from values/ in English; only layout direction and date/number formatting change.

  What replaces ar-XB's safety. Its one advantage was that no real phone can be set to it,
  so the build type could never be mistaken for a translation. That is now guarded properly
  instead of incidentally, and in three places that are all stronger than a scary-looking
  tag: the build type carries applicationIdSuffix ".rtl" so it installs beside the real app
  rather than over it; app_name says "SajdaTime (RTL)"; and NoTranslationsYetTest fails the
  build if any values-<lang>/ folder appears anywhere in the project. That last one guards
  the actual hard rule in CLAUDE.md — never machine-translate the app — which ar-XB never
  did: nothing about a pseudolocale ever stopped someone adding values-ar/ to main.

  Two neater-looking approaches were tried first and both failed, so do not "tidy" this back
  into either:

  - isPseudoLocalesEnabled = true on the debug build type. Generates en-XA and ar-XB, and
    would also have padded every string ~30% longer to catch labels that only fit because
    English is short. But aapt2 pseudolocalises the default strings at compile time and its
    generated value beats an explicit one, so app_language_tag arrived as the pseudolocalised
    text "&#8207;&#8238;en-GB&#8237;&#8207;", which parses to no locale at all. Confirmed by
    dumping the built APK's resource table.
  - A real values-ar-rXB/ resource folder in a debug source set. Works, and is the obvious
    way to do it — but lint reads any values-ar* folder as an Arabic translation and reports
    every string in the app as MissingTranslation. tools:ignore on that file does not help,
    because lint raises the error against the default strings.xml, not the translation. The
    only fixes left were disabling MissingTranslation or scoping it away, and both would
    disarm the check that actually matters on the day a real, possibly incomplete values-ar/
    lands. A build type has no locale folder, so lint has nothing to misread.
-->
<resources>
    <string name="app_language_tag">ur</string>
    <!-- The build type carries applicationIdSuffix ".rtl" so this sits beside the Play
         build rather than replacing it. Two SajdaTime icons on one launcher are otherwise
         indistinguishable, and installing the RTL preview over the real app is the mistake
         the suffix exists to prevent. -->
    <string name="app_name">SajdaTime (RTL)</string>
</resources>
